    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that colimits in $\calX $ are universal. According to Theorems <a href="/tag/5A32AA" data-tag="5A32AA">6.1.0.6</a> and <a href="/tag/53AECD" data-tag="53AECD">6.1.3.9</a>, $\calX $ is an $\infty $-topos if and only if the class $S$ consisting of all morphisms of $\calX $ is local. This clearly implies $(2)$, in view of Proposition <a href="/tag/2DA87A" data-tag="2DA87A">6.1.6.7</a>. Conversely, suppose that $(2)$ is satisfied, and let $S_{\kappa }$ be defined as in the statement of Proposition <a href="/tag/2DA87A" data-tag="2DA87A">6.1.6.7</a>. Proposition <a href="/tag/FEC8E8" data-tag="FEC8E8">6.1.6.3</a> ensures that $S_{\kappa }$ is local for all sufficiently large regular cardinals $\kappa $. We note that $S = \bigcup S_{\kappa }$. It follows from Criterion $(3)$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/C9D20A" data-tag="C9D20A">6.1.3.7</a> that $S$ is also local, so that $\calX $ is an $\infty $-topos. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
